// Title: Visual Studio Code Tunnel Execution
// Sigma ID: 90d6bd71-dffb-4989-8d86-a827fedd6624
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems), citron_ninja
// Date: 2023-10-25 | Modified: 2025-10-29
// ATT&CK: command-and-control, T1071.001, T1219
// Description: Detects Visual Studio Code tunnel execution. Attackers can abuse this functionality to establish
//   a C2 channel
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate use of Visual Studio Code tunnel
// References:
//   - https://ipfyx.fr/post/visual-studio-code-tunnel/
//   - https://badoption.eu/blog/2023/01/31/code_c2.html
//   - https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/remote/tunnels
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_vscode_tunnel_execution.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, microsoft_xdr schema)

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (isnull(ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName) and ProcessCommandLine endswith ".exe tunnel") or (ProcessCommandLine contains ".exe tunnel" and ProcessCommandLine contains "--accept-server-license-terms") or (InitiatingProcessCommandLine endswith " tunnel" and FolderPath endswith "\\cmd.exe" and (ProcessCommandLine contains "/d /c " and ProcessCommandLine contains "\\servers\\Stable-" and ProcessCommandLine contains "code-server.cmd"))
